.TH MINIOS-SESSION 1 "September 2026" "MiniOS Session Manager 2.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
minios-session \- command-line interface for managing MiniOS persistent sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B minios-session
[\fIGLOBAL_OPTIONS\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIcommand_options\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B minios-session
is a command-line utility for managing MiniOS persistent sessions from within the running system.
It provides full control over session operations including viewing, creating, activating, and deleting sessions.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output results in JSON format (can be used with any command)
.TP
.B \-\-sessions-dir \fIPATH\fR
Use custom sessions directory instead of default location
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List all available sessions with detailed information including ID, mode, version, edition, size, and last modification time.
.TP
.B active
Show information about the currently active session (session that will boot next).
.TP
.B running
Show information about the currently running session in this boot session.
.TP
.B info
Show filesystem type and compatible session modes for the active media.
.TP
.B activate \fIsession_id\fR
Activate the specified session by setting it as the default session. This change takes effect on the next boot.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to activate
.RE
.TP
.B create [\fImode\fR] [\fIsize\fR] [\-\-encryption none|luks] [\-\-compression CODEC] [\-\-activate]
Create a new session with the specified mode and optional size. The new session will be automatically assigned the next available ID. The tool automatically detects the filesystem type and only allows compatible session modes.
With --activate, the new session and its selection as boot default are published
together. The running session is not changed. Use --sessions-dir to create a
session on another mounted MiniOS installation.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-activate\fR (optional)
Select the completed session as the boot default in the same metadata update.
The running-session selector is preserved. Combine with --sessions-dir to
create and select a session on a target installation medium. Without this
option, the current boot default is unchanged.
.TP
\fImode\fR (optional)
Session mode: native, squashfs, dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, or raw (default: native). DynBlk is exposed only when its userspace utility, kernel module, and initrd capability marker are available.
.TP
\fIsize\fR (optional)
Decimal size in MB, GB, or TB for container modes. DynFileFS and raw
default to 4000MB. DynBlk defaults to 16GiB and uses the installed backend limit (dynblk limits).
.TP
\fB\-\-encryption\fR none|luks (optional)
Encrypt Raw, DynFileFS, or DynBlk storage with LUKS2. Encrypted creation reads
the passphrase and confirmation interactively or from standard input with
\fB\-\-password-stdin\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-compression\fR CODEC (optional)
DynBlk creation codec. Known codecs are none, lz4, lz4hc, lzo, lzo-rle, zstd,
deflate, and 842. Before creating storage, minios-session checks the running
kernel's kmod metadata under /run/initramfs and rejects codecs whose crypto_comp
provider is unavailable there. The option applies only to DynBlk and cannot be
combined with LUKS encryption.
.RE
.TP
.B delete \fIsession_id\fR
Delete the specified session by ID. Cannot delete the currently active session or currently running session.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to delete
.RE
.TP
.B resize \fIsession_id\fR \fIsize\fR
Resize the specified session to a larger size in MB, GB, or TB. Works for dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, and raw sessions, including their encrypted forms. DynBlk growth expands the virtual block device and then its ext4 filesystem without preallocating the new virtual capacity. Cannot resize currently running sessions.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to resize
.TP
\fIsize\fR (required)
New decimal size in MB, GB, or TB. Must be larger than current size.
.RE
.TP
.B export \fIsession_id\fR \fIoutput_path\fR [\-\-no-verify]
Export a session to a .tar.zst archive. Encrypted sessions are decrypted and exported
as logical files; the encrypted container is not copied into the archive.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to export
.TP
\fIoutput_path\fR (required)
Path to output file or directory. If directory is provided, filename is auto-generated.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-verify\fR (optional)
Skip archive verification after creation
.RE
.TP
.B import \fIarchive_path\fR [\-\-auto-convert] [\-\-force-mode \fIMODE\fR] [\-\-force-encryption none|luks] [\-\-compression CODEC] [\-\-skip-compatibility-check] [\-\-no-verify]
Import a session from a .tar.zst archive file. Supports automatic mode conversion.
.RS
.TP
\fIarchive_path\fR (required)
Path to the archive file (.tar.zst)
.TP
\fB\-\-auto-convert\fR (optional)
Automatically convert to compatible mode for current filesystem
.TP
\fB\-\-force-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR (optional)
Force import as a specific storage mode (native/dynfilefs/dynblk/vmdk/raw)
.TP
\fB\-\-force-encryption\fR none|luks (optional)
Force target encryption. A LUKS target receives a fresh LUKS header and ext4
identity.
.TP
\fB\-\-compression\fR CODEC (optional)
Select compression when the import target is DynBlk. Not available with LUKS.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-verify\fR (optional)
Skip session integrity verification after import
.TP
\fB\-\-skip-compatibility-check\fR (optional)
Skip source MiniOS version, edition, and union-filesystem compatibility checks.
.RE
.TP
.B copy \fIsession_id\fR [\-\-to-mode \fIMODE\fR] [\-\-to-encryption none|luks] [\-\-size \fISIZE\fR] [\-\-compression CODEC]
Logically copy a session through its filesystem, optionally changing storage,
capacity, or encryption. The target receives fresh filesystem and LUKS identities.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to copy
.TP
\fB\-\-to-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR (optional)
Convert to a different storage mode: native, dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, or raw
.TP
\fB\-\-to-encryption\fR none|luks (optional)
Select target encryption.
.TP
\fB\-\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR (optional)
Decimal size in MB, GB, or TB for dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, or raw targets.
DynBlk defaults to 16GiB; the other container modes default to 4000MB when no source size is inferred.
.TP
\fB\-\-compression\fR CODEC (optional)
Select compression for a newly created DynBlk copy target. Not available with LUKS.
.RE
.TP
.B clone \fIsession_id\fR
Physically clone a detached session backend. The clone preserves LUKS headers,
keyslots, LUKS UUID, and ext4 UUID and does not require a passphrase.
.TP
.B convert \fIsession_id\fR \fItarget_mode\fR [\-\-to-encryption none|luks] [\-\-size \fISIZE\fR] [\-\-compression CODEC] [\-\-new-session]
Convert an existing session to a different mode in-place.
.RS
.TP
\fIsession_id\fR (required)
ID of the session to convert
.TP
\fItarget_mode\fR (required)
Target storage mode: native, dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, or raw
.TP
\fB\-\-to-encryption\fR none|luks (optional)
Select target encryption.
.TP
\fB\-\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR (optional)
Decimal size in MB, GB, or TB for dynfilefs, dynblk, vmdk, or raw targets.
.TP
\fB\-\-compression\fR CODEC (optional)
Select compression for the new DynBlk container. Not available with LUKS.
.TP
\fB\-\-new-session\fR (optional)
Create a converted session with a new ID and preserve the source. Without this
option conversion replaces the source in-place.
.RE
.TP
.B cleanup [\-\-days \fIN\fR]
Delete all sessions older than N days. The currently active session and currently running session are never deleted. If called without parameters, uses default threshold of 30 days.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-\-days\fR \fIN\fR (optional)
Age threshold in days for session deletion (default: 30). Sessions modified within this period are preserved.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Check sessions directory status and write permissions. Reports filesystem type and whether session operations are available.
.SH COMMAND BEHAVIOR
.TP
.B Filesystem Compatibility Checking
The
.B create
command automatically detects the filesystem type and validates mode compatibility. If the requested mode is not supported:
.RS
.TP
- On FAT32/NTFS/exFAT with native mode: Returns an error suggesting a container mode
.TP
- On read-only media: Returns error stating no sessions can be created
.TP
- With dynfilefs mode but package not installed: Returns error requesting dynfilefs package installation
.TP
- With DynBlk mode but no matching initrd/kernel capability: Returns an availability error
.TP
- With an oversized raw image on FAT32: Returns an error with the 4000MB limit
.RE
.TP
.B Default Parameter Behavior
.RS
.TP
- 
.B create
without mode: Uses native mode, may fail on non-POSIX filesystems
.TP
- 
.B create
without size: Uses 16GiB for DynBlk/VMDK and 4000MB for dynfilefs and raw modes
.TP
- 
.B cleanup
without days: Uses 30-day threshold for session deletion
.RE
.TP
.B Session Protection
Active sessions are protected from deletion and in-place conversion. Running
sessions are protected from deletion, export, copy, resize, and conversion.
.SH SESSION MODES
MiniOS supports six persistence storage modes:
.TP
.B native
Direct storage on POSIX-compatible filesystems (ext4, btrfs, etc.). This is the default and most efficient mode for compatible filesystems.
.TP
.B dynfilefs
Dynamic file-based storage suitable for non-POSIX filesystems (FAT32, NTFS, exFAT). Creates expandable container files.
.TP
.B dynblk
Format-1 kernel block storage backed by thin volumeNNN.db files. The mode is available only when the running system has the dynblk utility and module and the initramfs advertises dynblk resume support. Its virtual size defaults to 16GiB and is limited by the installed backend (dynblk limits).
.TP
.B vmdk
Standard split sparse VMDK storage exposed through the DynBlk driver. It uses the same virtual-size policy as DynBlk and does not support backend compression.
.TP
.B raw
Fixed-size image file storage compatible with any filesystem. Requires pre-allocation of storage space.
.TP
.B Encryption
Raw, DynFileFS, DynBlk, and VMDK may optionally use LUKS2 encryption. Passphrases are
read interactively or from standard input with \fB--password-stdin\fR and are
never recorded in metadata. Encrypted-to-encrypted logical copy and conversion
read the source passphrase first, followed by the target passphrase twice.
.PP
LUKS choices and --password-stdin are shown only when the selected backend tools
are available and /run/initramfs/etc/minios-initramfs-crypt contains
luks-layer-v1.
.SH FILESYSTEM COMPATIBILITY
The tool automatically detects your media's filesystem type and restricts session creation to compatible modes:
.TP
.B POSIX Filesystems (ext2/3/4, btrfs, xfs, f2fs)
Native mode and available container modes are supported. DynBlk is admitted on ext2/3/4 and Btrfs when its capability check succeeds; XFS/f2fs are not DynBlk backing targets. Encryption additionally
requires cryptsetup and the backend-specific initrd capabilities.
.TP
.B FAT32, NTFS, and exFAT
DynFileFS and Raw are supported on writable media. DynBlk and VMDK are also offered on vfat, exFAT, and ntfs3 when their capability checks succeed. Encryption is supported
when its backend-specific capability check succeeds; fixed Raw files are limited
to 4000MB on FAT32 whether encrypted or not. Native mode is unsupported.
.TP
.B Read-only Media
No session creation possible. Sessions can only be activated or listed.
.PP
Under UEFI Secure Boot, DynBlk and VMDK creation is disabled because MiniOS does not sign the external DynBlk kernel module.
.PP
Use the
.B info
command to check filesystem compatibility before creating sessions.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
List all sessions:
.RS
.B minios-session list
.RE
.PP
Show active session:
.RS
.B minios-session active
.RE
.PP
Activate session:
.RS
.B minios-session activate 2
.RE
.PP
Delete old sessions:
.RS
.B minios-session cleanup --days 7
.RE
.PP
Create sessions:
.RS
.B minios-session create
.br
.B minios-session create native
.br
.B minios-session create dynfilefs 8000
.br
.B minios-session create dynblk 16GB
.br
.B minios-session create raw 2000
.RE
.PP
Resize session:
.RS
.B minios-session resize 2 8000
.RE
.PP
Export session:
.RS
.B minios-session export 3 /tmp/backup.tar.zst
.br
.B minios-session export 3 /tmp/
.RE
.PP
Import session:
.RS
.B minios-session import /tmp/backup.tar.zst
.br
.B minios-session import /tmp/backup.tar.zst --force-mode dynblk
.br
.B minios-session import /tmp/backup.tar.zst --auto-convert
.RE
.PP
Copy session:
.RS
.B minios-session copy 2
.br
.B minios-session copy 2 --to-mode raw --size 2000
.RE
.PP
Convert session:
.RS
.B minios-session convert 3 dynblk --size 16GB
.RE
.PP
JSON output:
.RS
.B minios-session --json list
.RE
.PP
Check directory status:
.RS
.B minios-session status
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /run/initramfs/memory/data/minios/changes/
Primary location for session data and metadata.
.TP
.B /run/initramfs/memory/data/minios/changes/session.json
Session metadata in JSON format (preferred).
.TP
.B /run/initramfs/memory/data/minios/changes/session.conf
Session metadata in legacy format (fallback).
.TP
.B /run/initramfs/memory/data/minios/changes/[0-9]+/
Individual session directories containing persistent changes.
.TP
.B /etc/minios-release
System version and edition information used for session compatibility checking.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error occurred (invalid arguments, session not found, permission denied, etc.)
.SH NOTES
.PP
Session activation takes effect on the next boot. The tool automatically detects session storage location and requests authentication via PolicyKit when needed.
